[{:db/id "ce7d179e-f508-591e-acc5-c6afaa82a765" :answer/id #uuid "ce7d179e-f508-591e-acc5-c6afaa82a765" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "25429db6-9731-5cad-93b2-3769a934d1b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "25429db6-9731-5cad-93b2-3769a934d1b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["ce7d179e-f508-591e-acc5-c6afaa82a765"] :answer-field/correct "ce7d179e-f508-591e-acc5-c6afaa82a765" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "56185f3f-fc8b-55b9-9a8b-a974adfba2df" :answer/id #uuid "56185f3f-fc8b-55b9-9a8b-a974adfba2df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "496f73d6-8ab7-51ed-9199-483eb4b51393" :answer-field/id #uuid "496f73d6-8ab7-51ed-9199-483eb4b51393" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["56185f3f-fc8b-55b9-9a8b-a974adfba2df"] :answer-field/correct "56185f3f-fc8b-55b9-9a8b-a974adfba2df" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "160d6d55-a68a-5cd0-a8b9-c87c2fd8df95" :question/id #uuid "160d6d55-a68a-5cd0-a8b9-c87c2fd8df95" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{6})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{\\pi}{6})=(2,\\frac{\\pi}{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi}{6}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\   & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi}{6}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["25429db6-9731-5cad-93b2-3769a934d1b3" "496f73d6-8ab7-51ed-9199-483eb4b51393"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "394e6e13-81f4-56a2-910a-dedf04a04292" :answer/id #uuid "394e6e13-81f4-56a2-910a-dedf04a04292" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "86a1a259-b391-5a29-8850-bdded74725d7" :answer-field/id #uuid "86a1a259-b391-5a29-8850-bdded74725d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["394e6e13-81f4-56a2-910a-dedf04a04292"] :answer-field/correct "394e6e13-81f4-56a2-910a-dedf04a04292" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "25037821-3854-562c-994e-f296a336d4d5" :answer/id #uuid "25037821-3854-562c-994e-f296a336d4d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "9e034bc3-d723-558a-a6dd-0121b631b793" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e034bc3-d723-558a-a6dd-0121b631b793" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["25037821-3854-562c-994e-f296a336d4d5"] :answer-field/correct "25037821-3854-562c-994e-f296a336d4d5" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "09b619c0-b536-5243-a608-187bb7fdbae4" :question/id #uuid "09b619c0-b536-5243-a608-187bb7fdbae4" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{\\pi}{3})=(2,\\frac{\\pi}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi}{3}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi}{3}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\   & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["86a1a259-b391-5a29-8850-bdded74725d7" "9e034bc3-d723-558a-a6dd-0121b631b793"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "5693ed90-3135-59e7-85d8-eee8e7d665c9" :answer/id #uuid "5693ed90-3135-59e7-85d8-eee8e7d665c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "67693190-4101-5007-a37d-f3b8bec0edf9" :answer-field/id #uuid "67693190-4101-5007-a37d-f3b8bec0edf9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["5693ed90-3135-59e7-85d8-eee8e7d665c9"] :answer-field/correct "5693ed90-3135-59e7-85d8-eee8e7d665c9" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "5fb777fb-61ce-52be-bae8-b3c80108daf8" :answer/id #uuid "5fb777fb-61ce-52be-bae8-b3c80108daf8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "64db8c78-ed3e-59c4-85d7-44111136c2f8" :answer-field/id #uuid "64db8c78-ed3e-59c4-85d7-44111136c2f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["5fb777fb-61ce-52be-bae8-b3c80108daf8"] :answer-field/correct "5fb777fb-61ce-52be-bae8-b3c80108daf8" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "20264f6c-2c36-5db7-805a-4dc03a3191d1" :question/id #uuid "20264f6c-2c36-5db7-805a-4dc03a3191d1" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{2\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{2\\pi}{3})=(2,\\frac{2\\pi}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\   & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\   & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-1,\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["67693190-4101-5007-a37d-f3b8bec0edf9" "64db8c78-ed3e-59c4-85d7-44111136c2f8"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "0bbdaaf9-dc0c-5d8d-8232-0e7af48e54c6" :answer/id #uuid "0bbdaaf9-dc0c-5d8d-8232-0e7af48e54c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c6d5fcdd-fca0-5966-aad3-6df23b58197c" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6d5fcdd-fca0-5966-aad3-6df23b58197c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["0bbdaaf9-dc0c-5d8d-8232-0e7af48e54c6"] :answer-field/correct "0bbdaaf9-dc0c-5d8d-8232-0e7af48e54c6" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "65f169b7-0532-51cd-8478-f1a94bd41ed7" :answer/id #uuid "65f169b7-0532-51cd-8478-f1a94bd41ed7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "72486232-f6d3-5b62-b9a2-3e7e4f12cadb" :answer-field/id #uuid "72486232-f6d3-5b62-b9a2-3e7e4f12cadb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["65f169b7-0532-51cd-8478-f1a94bd41ed7"] :answer-field/correct "65f169b7-0532-51cd-8478-f1a94bd41ed7" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "7423cb94-dc90-5a23-868c-cd90a0dda4e8" :question/id #uuid "7423cb94-dc90-5a23-868c-cd90a0dda4e8" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{5\\pi}{6})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{5\\pi}{6})=(2,\\frac{5\\pi}{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\   & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["c6d5fcdd-fca0-5966-aad3-6df23b58197c" "72486232-f6d3-5b62-b9a2-3e7e4f12cadb"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "305b90e4-d28c-5e61-ba1a-abab0ef30825" :answer/id #uuid "305b90e4-d28c-5e61-ba1a-abab0ef30825" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c68f7ff0-3bce-517b-b69e-7e9ece13716a" :answer-field/id #uuid "c68f7ff0-3bce-517b-b69e-7e9ece13716a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["305b90e4-d28c-5e61-ba1a-abab0ef30825"] :answer-field/correct "305b90e4-d28c-5e61-ba1a-abab0ef30825" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "be90888d-34e7-51c7-b007-7c1341e8a537" :answer/id #uuid "be90888d-34e7-51c7-b007-7c1341e8a537" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "a1b43544-5038-5a80-abd2-bcf94d3add9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "a1b43544-5038-5a80-abd2-bcf94d3add9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["be90888d-34e7-51c7-b007-7c1341e8a537"] :answer-field/correct "be90888d-34e7-51c7-b007-7c1341e8a537" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "83a86207-91b3-5107-a861-0feb8d90da51" :question/id #uuid "83a86207-91b3-5107-a861-0feb8d90da51" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{7\\pi}{6})=(2,\\frac{7\\pi}{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\   & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\   & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["c68f7ff0-3bce-517b-b69e-7e9ece13716a" "a1b43544-5038-5a80-abd2-bcf94d3add9b"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "fac864e5-68ed-527c-bcb5-5a626ad820e2" :answer/id #uuid "fac864e5-68ed-527c-bcb5-5a626ad820e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "3c886efd-dc0a-54b5-98ce-ffe60d60ea44" :answer/id #uuid "3c886efd-dc0a-54b5-98ce-ffe60d60e